# Vercel Web Analytics Implementation

Successfully implemented Vercel Web Analytics for the SciNets Discovery Hub project.

## Changes Made

### 1. Package Installation
- Installed `@vercel/analytics@^1.6.1` using npm
- Package added to `dependencies` in package.json
- Updated package-lock.json with the new dependency

### 2. Code Changes

#### Modified: src/App.tsx
- **Added import**: `import { Analytics } from "@vercel/analytics/react";`
- **Added component**: Placed `<Analytics />` component within the TooltipProvider, after the AuthProvider section
- This ensures analytics tracking is initialized at the root level of the application and will track all route changes

### Implementation Details

The Analytics component was added to the main App component using the React/Vite implementation pattern as specified in the Vercel Web Analytics documentation. The component is placed at the root level of the application to ensure:

1. Analytics tracking is active across all routes
2. Page views and navigation events are captured automatically
3. The tracking script loads efficiently without blocking the UI

### Testing Performed

1. ✅ **Build Test**: Successfully ran `npm run build` - production build completed without errors
2. ✅ **Linter Test**: Ran `npm run lint` - no new linting errors introduced in App.tsx
3. ✅ **Dependency Installation**: Verified @vercel/analytics package is properly installed

### Next Steps

To complete the Vercel Web Analytics setup:

1. Deploy the application to Vercel using `vercel deploy`
2. Enable Web Analytics in the Vercel dashboard:
   - Go to the project on Vercel dashboard
   - Click the "Analytics" tab
   - Click "Enable" to activate analytics
3. After deployment, verify tracking is working by:
   - Visiting the deployed site
   - Opening browser DevTools Network tab
   - Looking for a request to `/_vercel/insights/view`
4. View analytics data in the Vercel dashboard after users visit the site

### Notes

- The implementation follows the official Vercel documentation for React/Vite applications
- No route support configuration is needed as the Analytics component automatically detects React Router route changes
- Pre-existing linting errors in other files were not addressed as they are outside the scope of this implementation
